iti96:2.3 | S: One unyoked from both sensual pleasures and rebirth is a perfected one, who has ended defilements.” | T: Untied from [both] the yoke of sensuality & from the yoke of becoming, one is an Arahant whose effluents are ended.”
iti96:2.4 | S: The Buddha spoke this matter. | T: (none)
iti96:2.5 | S: On this it is said: | T: (none)
iti96:3.1 | S: “Yoked to both sensual pleasures | T: Tied by both the yoke of sensuality
iti96:3.2 | S: and the desire to be reborn in a future life; | T: & the yoke of becoming,
iti96:3.3 | S: sentient beings continue to transmigrate, | T: beings go to the wandering-on
iti96:3.4 | S: with ongoing birth and death. | T: leading to birth & death.
iti96:4.1 | S: Those who’ve given up sensual pleasures | T: Those who’ve abandoned the sensual
iti96:4.2 | S: without attaining the end of defilements, | T: without reaching the ending of effluents,
iti96:4.3 | S: and are still yoked to being reborn, | T: are tied by the yoke of becoming,
iti96:4.4 | S: are said to be non-returners. | T: are said to be Non-returners.
iti96:5.1 | S: Those who have cut off doubt, | T: While those who’ve cut off doubt
iti96:5.2 | S: and ended conceit and future lives, | T: have no more conceit or further becoming.
iti96:5.3 | S: they’re the ones in this world <j>who’ve truly crossed over, | T: They who have reached the ending of effluents, while in the world have gone beyond.
iti96:5.4 | S: having reached the ending of defilements.” | T: (none)
iti96:6.1 | S: This too is a matter that was spoken by the Blessed One: that is what I heard. | T: (none)
iti96:6.2 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)
iti96:6.3 | P: Tatiyabhāṇavāraṁ. | T: (none)

## sn51.20
sn51.20:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, when the four bases of psychic power are developed and cultivated they’re very fruitful and beneficial. | T: “These four bases of power, when developed & pursued, are of great fruit & great benefit.
sn51.20:2.1 | S: How so? | T: And how are the four bases of power developed & pursued so as to be of great fruit & great benefit?
sn51.20:2.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant develops the basis of psychic power that has immersion due to enthusiasm, and active effort. | T: “There is the case where a monk develops the base of power endowed with concentration founded on desire & the fabrications of exertion,
sn51.20:2.3 | S: They think: ‘My enthusiasm won’t be too lax or too tense. And it’ll be neither constricted internally nor scattered externally.’ | T: thinking, ‘This desire of mine will be neither overly sluggish nor overly active, neither inwardly constricted nor outwardly scattered.’
sn51.20:2.4 | S: And they meditate perceiving before and behind: | T: He keeps perceiving what is in front & behind so that what is in front is the same as what is behind,
sn51.20:2.5 | S: as before, so behind; as behind, so before; | T: what is behind is the same as what is in front.
sn51.20:2.6 | S: as below, so above; as above, so below; | T: What is below is the same as what is above, what is above is the same as what is below.
sn51.20:2.7 | S: as by day, so by night; as by night, so by day. | T: (He dwells) by night as by day, and by day as by night.
sn51.20:2.8 | S: And so, with an open and unenveloped heart, they develop a mind that’s full of radiance. | T: By means of an awareness thus open & unhampered, he develops a brightened mind.
sn51.20:2.9 | S: They develop the basis of psychic power that has immersion due to energy … | T: “He develops the base of power endowed with concentration founded on persistence…
sn51.20:2.10 | S: mental development … | T: “He develops the base of power endowed with concentration founded on intent…
sn51.20:2.11 | S: inquiry, and active effort. | T: “He develops the base of power endowed with concentration founded on discrimination & the fabrications of exertion,
sn51.20:2.12 | S: They think: ‘My inquiry won’t be too lax or too tense. And it’ll be neither constricted internally nor scattered externally.’ | T: thinking, ‘This discrimination of mine will be neither overly sluggish nor overly active, neither inwardly constricted nor outwardly scattered.’
sn51.20:2.13 | S: And they meditate perceiving before and behind: | T: He keeps perceiving what is in front & behind so that what is in front is the same as what is behind,
sn51.20:2.14 | S: as before, so behind; as behind, so before; | T: what is behind is the same as what is in front.
sn51.20:2.15 | S: as below, so above; as above, so below; | T: What is below is the same as what is above, what is above is the same as what is below.
sn51.20:2.16 | S: as by day, so by night; as by night, so by day. | T: (He dwells) by night as by day, and by day as by night.
sn51.20:2.17 | S: And so, with an open and unenveloped heart, they develop a mind that’s full of radiance. | T: By means of an awareness thus open & unhampered, he develops a brightened mind.
sn51.20:3.1 | S: And what is enthusiasm that’s too lax? | T: “And how is desire overly sluggish?
sn51.20:3.2 | S: It’s when enthusiasm is coupled with laziness. | T: Whatever desire is accompanied by laziness, conjoined with laziness,
sn51.20:3.3 | S: This is called lax enthusiasm. | T: that is called overly sluggish desire.
sn51.20:4.1 | S: And what is enthusiasm that’s too tense? | T: “And how is desire overly active?
sn51.20:4.2 | S: It’s when enthusiasm is coupled with restlessness. | T: Whatever desire is accompanied by restlessness, conjoined with restlessness,
sn51.20:4.3 | S: This is called tense enthusiasm. | T: that is called overly active desire.
sn51.20:5.1 | S: And what is enthusiasm that’s constricted internally? | T: “And how is desire inwardly constricted?
sn51.20:5.2 | S: It’s when enthusiasm is coupled with dullness and drowsiness. | T: Whatever desire is accompanied by sloth & drowsiness, conjoined with sloth & drowsiness,
sn51.20:5.3 | S: This is called enthusiasm constricted internally. | T: that is called inwardly restricted desire.
sn51.20:6.1 | S: And what is enthusiasm that’s scattered externally? | T: “And how is desire outwardly scattered?
sn51.20:6.2 | S: It’s when enthusiasm is frequently scattered and diffused externally on account of the five kinds of sensual stimulation. | T: Whatever desire is stirred up by the five strands of sensuality, outwardly dispersed & dissipated,
sn51.20:6.3 | S: This is called enthusiasm scattered externally. | T: that is called outwardly scattered desire.
sn51.20:7.1 | S: And how does a mendicant meditate perceiving before and behind: | T: “And how does a monk dwell perceiving what is in front & behind so that what is in front is the same as what is behind,
sn51.20:7.2 | S: as before, so behind; as behind, so before? | T: and what is behind is the same as what is in front?
sn51.20:7.3 | S: It’s when the perception of before and behind is properly grasped, focused on, borne in mind, and penetrated with wisdom by a mendicant. | T: There is the case where a monk’s perception of what is in front & behind is well in hand, well-attended to, well-considered, well-tuned [‘penetrated’] by means of discernment.
sn51.20:7.4 | S: That’s how a mendicant meditates perceiving before and behind: | T: This is how a monk keeps perceiving what is in front and behind so that what is in front is the same as what is behind,
sn51.20:7.5 | S: as before, so behind; as behind, so before. | T: and what is behind is the same as what is in front.
sn51.20:8.1 | S: And how does a mendicant meditate as below, so above; as above, so below? | T: “And how does a monk dwell so that what is below is the same as what is above, and what is above is the same as what is below?
sn51.20:8.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant examines their own body up from the soles of the feet and down from the tips of the hairs, wrapped in skin and full of many kinds of filth. | T: There is the case where a monk reflects on this very body, from the soles of the feet on up, from the crown of the head on down, surrounded by skin, & full of various kinds of unclean things:
sn51.20:8.3 | S: ‘In this body there is head hair, body hair, nails, teeth, skin, flesh, sinews, bones, bone marrow, kidneys, heart, liver, diaphragm, spleen, lungs, intestines, mesentery, undigested food, feces, bile, phlegm, pus, blood, sweat, fat, tears, grease, saliva, snot, synovial fluid, urine.’ | T: ‘In this body there are head hairs, body hairs, nails, teeth, skin, flesh, tendons, bones, bone marrow, kidneys, heart, liver, pleura, spleen, lungs, large intestines, small intestines, gorge, feces, bile, phlegm, pus, blood, sweat, fat, tears, skin-oil, saliva, mucus, fluid in the joints, urine.’
sn51.20:8.4 | S: That’s how a mendicant meditates as below, so above; as above, so below. | T: This is how a monk dwells so that what is below is the same as what is above, and what is above is the same as what is below.
sn51.20:9.1 | S: And how does a mendicant meditate as by day, so by night; as by night, so by day? | T: “And how does a monk dwell by night as by day, and by day as by night?
sn51.20:9.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant develops the basis of psychic power that has immersion due to enthusiasm, and active effort, with the same features, attributes, and signs by day as by night. | T: There is the case where a monk at night develops the base of power endowed with concentration founded on desire & the fabrications of exertion by means of the same modes [permutations] & signs & themes that he uses by day,
sn51.20:9.3 | S: And they develop it with the same features, attributes, and signs by night as by day. | T: and by day he develops the base of power endowed with concentration founded on desire & the fabrications of exertion by means of the same modes & signs & themes that he uses by night.
sn51.20:9.4 | S: That’s how a mendicant meditates as by day, so by night; as by night, so by day. | T: This is how a monk dwells by night as by day, and by day as by night.
sn51.20:10.1 | S: And how, with an open and unenveloped heart, does a mendicant develop a mind that’s full of radiance? | T: “And how does a monk—by means of an awareness open & unhampered—develop a brightened mind?
sn51.20:10.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant has properly grasped the perception of light, and has properly grasped the perception of day. | T: There is the case where a monk has the perception of light, the perception of daytime [at any hour of the day] well in hand & well-established.
sn51.20:10.3 | S: That’s how, with an open and unenveloped heart, a mendicant develops a mind that’s full of radiance. | T: This is how a monk—by means of an awareness open & unhampered—develops a brightened mind.
